# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PRIVATE FULL-BACKUP REPOSITORY
#
#  This repo is a near-complete physical snapshot: vendored proxy trees and
#  node_modules are committed on purpose so a new machine can clone and run.
#  The rules below are the deliberate exceptions.
#
#  ⚠  KEEP THIS REPOSITORY PRIVATE. .env is no longer tracked, but its HISTORY
#     still holds live credentials — see the Credentials note further down.
#
#  A rule here does NOT untrack a file that is already tracked — git ignores
#  only apply to files it is not already following. Two rules below are in that
#  state on purpose, and say so, rather than looking like they work.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Nebula — the one companion proxy NOT kept in the working tree.
# It was 520 MB across 33,360 files (an Astro app whose pnpm node_modules is a
# symlink store that restores as broken stubs on a fresh Windows clone anyway),
# and it never shipped: companions only appear when PROXY_*_URL is set, which
# render.yaml never does, so the live site has always been GUST + Ultraviolet +
# Scramjet. Carrying half a gigabyte at HEAD to support a local-only engine
# wasn't worth it.
#
# Nothing is lost — every file is still in git history:
#     git checkout 07a2a6f7 -- Nebula
# Or fetch it fresh:  git clone https://github.com/NebulaServices/Nebula
# then `cd Nebula && pnpm install && pnpm run build`. tools/proxies.mjs detects
# the folder and starts it automatically if it's present and built.
Nebula/

# ── Regenerated caches ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-side thumbnail proxy cache (rebuilt on demand).
.cache/
# Y8 harvest scratch. It was tracked, which made it show as modified after any
# harvest run and put half a megabyte of churn in front of every real diff.
.y8-cache/

# Python bytecode cache (never useful in the repo; version-specific).
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Python virtual environment for tools/requirements.txt.
#
# `python -m venv` writes a .gitignore containing `*` INSIDE the directory, so
# this is already ignored without the line below. It is here anyway because that
# protection is a file inside the thing being protected: recreate the
# environment with a tool that does not write it, or copy the tree, and 143 MB
# of third-party packages becomes stageable by one `git add -A`.
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Supabase CLI local state (regenerated; never part of the repo).
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.branches/

# ── Tool output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Run logs. These were tracked, so the repo carried one machine's migration
# errors from July as though they were source.
logs/
*.log

# Backups written by tools/build-merged.mjs and tools/cleanup-ip.mjs before they
# rewrite data/top_games.json.
#
# Tracking these actively BROKE the safety net they exist to provide: both tools
# guard with `if (!existsSync(bak))`, so a committed .bak from July meant a run
# today skipped taking a backup and left the stale one in place. A backup is
# only useful if it is from immediately before the change it protects against.
*.bak

# ── Credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env is ignored AND untracked (commit 1dd45a7e), so local credentials stay
# local. It still exists on disk — untracking removed it from the index, not the
# filesystem. Production gets these from the host: render.yaml declares every
# secret the server reads, which is what makes untracking safe rather than a
# thing that breaks the deploy.
#
# THE HISTORY STILL HOLDS LIVE SECRETS — accepted, not outstanding.
# Untracking removed .env from HEAD, not from history: `git show 45821901:.env`
# still returns it. Verified 2026-08-06 by comparing the working .env against
# every commit that ever touched it — the values CURRENTLY IN USE are
# byte-identical to committed ones for SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (an active
# sb_secret_… key that bypasses RLS entirely), ADMIN_SECRET, ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET
# (which also keys the audit log's IP HMAC), ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH and CRON_SECRET.
#
# An earlier note said history was "not a problem for the Supabase pair" because
# the LEGACY anon/service_role JWTs are deactivated. True, and beside the point:
# the key in use today is the new-format secret key, and it is in history too.
#
# DECISION (owner, 2026-08-06): this repository is private and will never be made
# public, so rotation is not being done. Recorded here so it is a decision with a
# reason rather than an oversight someone re-raises every audit.
#
# What that leaves, so the next reader can judge it for themselves rather than
# re-derive it:
#   · Every clone ever made still holds these values, and always will. Rotation
#     is the only thing that expires them; deleting or rewriting history is not.
#   · It couples two blast radii that are otherwise separate. A GitHub account or
#     third-party-integration compromise (Render has repo access) stops being
#     "someone read my source" and becomes "someone holds the production database
#     key and the admin credentials".
# Neither depends on the repo ever going public. If that trade stops looking
# right, rotate at source (Supabase → Settings → API Keys; `npm run admin-hash`
# for the admin trio; any random string for CRON_SECRET), set the new values in
# the HOST dashboard, and clear them from this file so it cannot re-supply a
# stale one. SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY alone is the two-minute version and removes most
# of the coupling above.
.env

# node_modules is committed deliberately (see the header), so this rule does not
# untrack the ~2,500 files already following. It only stops NEW package
# directories being added by accident.
node_modules

# Browser-verification screenshots. Real evidence, but regenerated on every
# run and not something the deploy needs. Retention is handled by the runner.
verification-evidence/
